            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Divine justice, the ultimate eschatological evaluation of deeds, the separation of the righteous from the wicked, and the final rectification of all wrongs.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Matthew 25:31-46\",\"Revelation 20:11-15\",\"2 Corinthians 5:10\",\"Daniel 7:9-10\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Historical, temporal punishment or wrath poured out on rebellious nations or individuals.\",\"basis\":[\"Amos 1:1-15\",\"Revelation 16:1-7\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Fatherly discipline and purification of believers or the church.\",\"basis\":[\"1 Peter 4:17\",\"1 Corinthians 11:32\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Vindication, salvation, and deliverance of the oppressed.\",\"basis\":[\"Judges 2:16-18\",\"Psalm 72:1-4\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Biblical symbolism for judgment often employs imagery of fire, scales, thrones, and agricultural harvests (separating wheat from chaff). While typically associated with condemnation in popular theology, in biblical studies it is frequently recognized as a positive restorative concept of setting the world right and rescuing the oppressed.\"}",
